{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 47, :comments ["<b>To perform the service of service.</b> This is the song, which was a service to another service, namely the offerings; and thus it says (Psalms 100:2): ״עבדו את ה׳ בשמחה״ — and the verse is complete, calling the song 'service'; as it is written in Chronicles (II 35): ״והמשוררים בני אסף על מעמדם אין להם לסור מעל עבודתם״ — and this song was with lyres and cymbals, and they would recite songs and hymns to the Holy One, blessed be He, at the time of the offering. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: how many strings were in the lyre? Seven, as it says (Psalms 16:11): ״שובע שמחות את פניך״ — do not read שובע but שבע (seven); and so David, peace be upon him, says (ibid. 119): ״שבע ביום הללתיך״; and in the days of the Messiah, eight, as it says (ibid. 12): ״למנצח על השמינית״ — on the eighth string. And in the world to come, ten, as it says (ibid. 33): ״בנבל עשור זמרו לו״, and it says (ibid. 92): ״עלי עשור ועלי נבל״; thus did our Rabbis of blessed memory interpret in tractate Arakhin, in the chapter 'Ein be-Arakhin,' at its end. And the explanation of the matter is that this world is under seven planets, and in the days of the Messiah attainment will increase with an added degree, and in the world to come attainment will be at perfection in the degree that is the totality of all degrees. And know that the matter of song was not specified until our master David, peace be upon him, came." "<b>And behold.</b> It is divided into eight parts, for the number of the Levite singers is eight parts, and they are: Asaph, Heman, Jeduthun, Ethan the Ezrahite — behold, four; and Levi was a name whose name appears as 'son' — behold, five; and 'Al Ha-Gittith' is the Gittite family, and it is the family of Obed-Edom — behold, six; and the sons of Korah — behold, seven; and the sons of Moses — behold, eight, for ״תפלה למשה״ concerns the sons of Moses. And they would also sing with eight instruments, and these are: 'Al Neginot,' 'Al Machalat,' 'Al Alamot Shir,' and 'Ha-Massa,' as it says (I Chronicles 15:22): ״וכנניהו שר הלוים במשא יסור במשא כי מבין הוא״ — and it is a strong and mighty sound, from the root of (Genesis 29:11): ״וישא את קולו״; and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in tractate Arakhin: do not read יסור but ישיר במשא, as it is written: לעבוד עבודת עבודה ועבודת משא. And 'Al Ha-Nechilot,' and it is from the root of חליל, as it is written (I Kings 1:40): ״והעם מחללים בחלילים״; and 'Al Shoshanim,' 'Al Ha-Gittith,' 'Al Ha-Sheminit.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "Source cites Chronicles II 35 without verse number; rabbinic allusion to II Chronicles 35:15. The citation תהילים ק׳:ב׳ is Psalm 100:2 as rendered.", 1 "The lemma והנה appears to be a continuation marker from the preceding comment's closing flow into a new section; 'Ha-Massa' and 'Al Ha-Nechilot' etc. refer to the superscriptions of Psalms."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:34.771024152Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}